    <h2>Background</h2>
    <p>I am an established real estate professional who has worked in both developer and agency roles, which has given me wide experience in land purchase, development, policy change and the many related areas around them. I also conduct seminars and training on policy and regulatory work, as well as project introductions, and I do collective sales and consultancy work with developers.</p>

    <h2>Why photography matters here</h2>
    <p>I am an avid photographer, and my love and training in the arts have earned me a different perspective when it comes to property reviews. Perhaps I see something that was overlooked.</p>
    <p>Every photograph on this site is mine. Not renders supplied by a developer, not stock &mdash; what the place actually looks like when you are standing in it, at the hour I happened to be standing there.</p>

    <blockquote class="pull">I am not describing a project from the outside. I have been in the room where ones like it were priced and planned.</blockquote>

    <h2>How I review</h2>
    <p>I visit the show gallery, usually before public launch. I walk the surrounding streets and look at the approach road. I examine the unit layouts and the fittings, and I read the development details and the URA maps rather than the brochure. Where the history of a place matters &mdash; and in Singapore it often does &mdash; I go and find it.</p>
    <p>Then every review ends the same way: what works, what does not, and who should look elsewhere. No development suits everybody, and a review that pretends otherwise is an advertisement.</p>
  </article>

